# =============================================================================
# _headers — Netlify response headers
# =============================================================================
# Purpose: the installed app must always show the latest published version.
#
# There is no service worker, so nothing caches pages on the device. What CAN
# still serve something stale is the browser's own HTTP cache: without an
# explicit instruction a browser is free to reuse a page it fetched earlier,
# which is how an app icon can open yesterday's version of the site.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` says: you may keep a copy, but ask before you
# use it. Netlify answers that question with an ETag, so an unchanged page
# comes back as an empty 304 rather than a full download. Opening the app
# stays fast and is guaranteed current.
#
# This is deliberately applied to EVERYTHING rather than just HTML. The CSS
# and JS filenames here don't carry content hashes — main.css is always
# main.css — so caching them aggressively would mean a design change silently
# not arriving. Revalidation costs one small request each.
#
# NOTE: this file starts with an underscore, so Jekyll ignores it by default.
# It is listed under `include:` in _config.yml to get copied into the build.
# =============================================================================

/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  # Don't let a browser second-guess the Content-Type we send. Without this,
  # a file that sniffs like HTML can be treated as HTML no matter what it was
  # served as — the classic way an uploaded image turns into a script.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # Send the full URL as referrer only within our own site; other sites see
  # just the origin. Keeps draft or unlisted URLs out of other people's logs.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # Nobody needs to frame the blog. /admin/ is separately set to DENY in
  # netlify.toml, which is stricter and stays that way.
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  # The site asks for none of these, so no page on it can quietly start.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content Security Policy — written out, deliberately not switched on
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A CSP lists the only places a page may load code, styles, images and fonts
# from. Anything else is blocked, which is what limits the damage when a third
# party gets compromised — the CARTO watermark was a reminder that outside
# services change without asking.
#
# It is commented out because a CSP that is even slightly wrong breaks the
# site quietly: the page loads, and one feature (photos, comments, the map)
# stops working with only a console message to say why. Turning it on is a
# deliberate ten-minute job, not a change to make blind.
#
# The list below covers what the site actually used as of 2026-08-27:
#   unpkg.com            Leaflet
#   cdn.jsdelivr.net     GLightbox
#   identity.netlify.com Netlify Identity (the /login/ page and /admin/)
#   cusdis.com           comments
#   follow.it            the subscribe button
#   fonts.googleapis.com / fonts.gstatic.com   Lora
#   res.cloudinary.com   every photo
#   upload.wikimedia.org the country and US state flags on /stats/
#   *.basemaps.cartocdn.com / services.arcgisonline.com   map tiles
#   img.youtube.com, www.youtube.com   video thumbnails and embeds
#
# TO TURN IT ON: uncomment the four lines, deploy, then walk a post with
# photos, /map/, /stats/, /search/, the comment box and /admin/ with the
# browser console open. Anything blocked names the host it wanted; add that
# host to the right list and deploy again. `unsafe-inline` is in there
# because the pages carry inline <script> and style="" attributes — removing
# that need is a much bigger change than this file.
#
#  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://unpkg.com https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://identity.netlify.com https://cusdis.com https://follow.it https://www.youtube.com https://media-library.cloudinary.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://unpkg.com https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://res.cloudinary.com https://upload.wikimedia.org https://*.basemaps.cartocdn.com https://services.arcgisonline.com https://server.arcgisonline.com https://tile.openstreetmap.org https://img.youtube.com https://unpkg.com; connect-src 'self' https://api.cloudinary.com https://cusdis.com https://api.follow.it; frame-src https://cusdis.com https://www.youtube.com https://media-library.cloudinary.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self' https://api.follow.it

# The manifest decides the app's name, icon and colours. A phone re-reads it
# when the app is installed and occasionally after; stale is harmless but
# pointless, so it follows the same rule.
/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json
